From 26716c135846904aacdb03fcd92c43c93fae576f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Blair DuCray-Oppat <251061449+blairducrayoppat@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 10:24:21 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] windows: `wfp status` reads `partial` when the set is
 incomplete, so a plain `install` repairs it

`filter_status` read `installed` whenever one `block-user` filter existed. With one address family's block deleted the set still read `installed`, `install` returned early with "already installed ... no changes", and that family stayed open; with the IPv4 block gone, the "Re-run `srt-win install`" that `initialize()` asks for then changed nothing.

- `installed` only when a `block-user` and a `permit-loopback-user` exist at both the v4 and v6 connect layers; `partial` when some of those are present; `absent` when none. Extra filters under the sublayer are still ignored. `port_range` comes from the first permit tag, as the comment says
- `install`: the early return applies only to `installed`, so a `partial` set is re-added. The config-drift refusal (exit 13 without --force) applies to a `partial` set too; a partial set with no permit left has no range to compare and falls through
- `WindowsWfpStatus` gains `partial`; `checkWindowsDependencies` names an incomplete set, with a fake-stdout test
- Unit test `fence_parts_state`; `note()` reads the layer names from `ALE_LAYERS`
- Measured on one Windows 11 build (26200), 3 of 3 per state, with the status change alone: every half-set read `partial`; after a plain `install`, `wfp status` read `installed` with filters=4 and `wfp verify` read blocked on 127.0.0.1 and [::1]
